Copies in Moth are always explicit. Ordinary assignment doesn't clone data.

### Making an independent copy

Use `copy` when you need a separate version of a value:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    original ~= {1, 2, 3}
    independent ~= copy original

    ~independent.push(4)
    -- original still has 3 items
]

Without `copy`, names can refer to the same collection. A mutable declaration
from an eligible mutable place creates a write-through alias:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    original ~= 10
    writer ~= original
    ~writer = 20  -- also changes original
]

### Mutating existing values with `~`

To change an existing value, use `~` before the value name:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    items ~= {1, 2, 3}
    ~items.push(4)
]

The `~` tells the compiler you want exclusive access to mutate the value.

### Fresh arguments don't need `~`

Pass a fresh value or an independent copy to a mutable parameter without `~`.
An existing mutable place needs the marker, including in a named argument:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    update(~items)
    update(value = ~items)
    update(copy items)
    update({1, 2, 3})
]

Mutable method receivers differ: they always need an existing mutable place,
as in `~items.push(4)`.
